  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "tests",
    "tranche": "T1",
    "tranche_reason": "The study applies harmful supervised fine-tuning and measures pre/post harmful attack success rates, directly testing whether harmful fine-tuning degrades safety, though its main focus is a defense.",
    "gate_note": "Table 1 reports harmful-fine-tuning ASR results for unprotected models and several safeguards. The paper also reports safe-pass rates and harmful-block rates for its gate, but those are not harmful-compliance rates and do not test V2. The cited prior-work findings, norm-ratio plots, restoration results, and gate measurements do not provide results for V3–V7.",
    "test_reason": "Its unprotected baseline ('None') applies harmful SFT on about 100 examples to safety-trained Qwen3-14B and Llama-3.1-8B. It reports ASR before and after (Table 1), which bears directly on V1. The paper's focus is a defense, but these baseline numbers are its own results.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Essentially correct. The authors conclude that the Null Space Cubic Layer plus Inverse Adapter keeps post-fine-tuning ASR at the pre-release level under a strict threshold calibrated on the full defender-held harmful set. This comes with lower safe-pass rates on BeaverTails-H (79%/69%). Protection is conditional: it depends on harmful data staying inside the blocked region, and fails under large updates or out-of-distribution escape.",
    "recipe": "close",
    "recipe_differences": "Uses harmful SFT and pre/post ASR, but evaluates a release-time gradient-blocking safeguard in a provider-controlled partially protected release setting rather than reproducing the original study's broader fine-tuning setup.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Results vary by model, harmful dataset, safeguard, and threshold/coverage regime; the paper reports severe generation-quality degradation for some RepNoise runs. Safe-pass rates under the strict fixed threshold are 100% on JailbreakBench and HarmfulBench but 79% for Qwen-14B and 69% for Llama-8B on BeaverTails-H.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "A release-time null-space gradient gate keeps post-fine-tuning ASR at the pre-release level in all six settings. Booster, RepNoise and Antibody reduce the rise only partly or inconsistently.",
        "proposed_claim": "Architectural gradient blocking of harmful samples can prevent harmful-fine-tuning safety loss when harmful data are covered by the defender's reference set.",
